[
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Shall there be an amendment to the Colorado Constitution creating a constitutional right to hunt, fish, and harvest fish and wildlife by traditional methods, including all species of fish and wildlife managed by the state except non-game species, endangered species, or any species that is illegal to hunt under federal law, and, in connection therewith, establishing hunting and fishing as the preferred means of managing fish and wildlife populations; and preserving the right of the state to regulate hunting, fishing, and wildlife management if necessary for sound scientific wildlife conservation and management, public safety, or to preserve the future of hunting and fishing opportunities for all species?",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Who put the measure on the ballot?",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Amendment 83 was placed on the ballot through a citizen initiative backed by operatives of ",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Advance Colorado",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://whoisadvancecolorado.com/"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": ", a right-wing group supporting numerous measures on Colorado’s 2026 ballot.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": null,
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Short Description:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Establishes a constitutional right to hunt, fish, and harvest fish and wildlife using traditional methods, and declares hunting and fishing the preferred means of managing fish and wildlife populations, while preserving specified state regulatory authority, private-property rights, and protections for nongame and endangered species. As a constitutional amendment, it requires at least 55% approval.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Analysis and Commentary:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Oppose. Amendment 83 was placed on the ballot through a citizen initiative backed by operatives of ",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Advance Colorado",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://whoisadvancecolorado.com/"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": ", a right-wing group supporting numerous measures on Colorado’s 2026 ballot.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Hunting and fishing is well-supported in Colorado. Recent state ",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "budgets for Parks and Wildlife",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://cpw.state.co.us/funding-colorado-parks-and-wildlife#4257225834-2043701162"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": " support both with tens of millions of dollars annually. There’s no threat of losing that support or ability for those activities. So why are Colorado voters being asked to add this amendment to the state constitution?",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "At this point in the ballot, you’re probably wondering why there are so many right-wing measures on the ballot in 2026. I’ve mentioned ",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Advance Colorado",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://whoisadvancecolorado.com/"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": ", but why did they put on so many measures? Taken as a whole, the measures are anti-LBGQTIA, anti-tax, anti-voter, pro-ICE, and pro-incarceration — with the goal of driving turnout for Republican candidates and measures. The reality is that Republicans and right-wing interests no longer have the ability to win a statewide race in Colorado, and may possibly become even less relevant while Democrats approach having supermajorities in both bodies of the state legislature. Given that, the only tool they still have is to put measure after measure on the state ballot in order to drive their main agendas of starving the state budget through tax policy and passing other harmful measures as they can muster it.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Back to Amendment 83, which attempts to drive voter turnout in a core constituency of Republican voters in a year that those voters are less-motivated to vote. Here are a few compelling arguments to vote the measure down:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "“Here’s the problem with Amendment 83: there are many other things people (including me) like to do outdoors, and this puts hunting above them all as the only one in our constitution. Coloradans hike, rock climb, mountain bike, camp, go river rafting and kayaking, ski, do trail running, go 4x4ing, and much more. While we do our best to support all of these wonderful ways to enjoy our great outdoors, elevating hunting above all the others means that when they come into conflict, hunting would always win out in the courts. So if there was a conflict between hunting and mountain biking, the courts would have to close off the area to mountain biking. Right now hunting has very strong legal protection in Colorado and that isn’t going anywhere. Putting this proposed language in the constitution is reckless and dangerous.”",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Governor Jared Polis on Facebook",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://www.facebook.com/PolisForColorado/posts/pfbid091SYmiVRAyiQDryBFoq8zT2ZLy7PooQu3UREtB87uSEWDFdQiznmtCDuPCo2y5Kml?__cft__[0]=AZjtomtmSa-93OgPelrmdp-_EQV2snC3zWzIRxdf0h1OX8jyoqcb9i57BEvYz0CDOirvKaFOW7F2-Vqf3DS8wTTt90Vspa3jFYCxXHYFuFA2hJb1vv_t0to9lq0cjZvX1hIoCUfNoD-dxI6-vc31T7rYZXeGTAgjydZdUBopdBnkhvhk9lCy&__tn__=%2CO%2CP-R"
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "“We kind of see this as a very right-wing, MAGA culture war sort of initiative,” said Eric Washburn, founder of  Colorado Hunters and Anglers for Sensible Wildlife Conservation. “[It] is really designed to repeal a lot of the wildlife reforms that Coloradans, whether it’s through voting or through the Colorado Parks and Wildlife Commission, have established over the last 40 or 50 years to make hunting in Colorado more ethical, and frankly, less cruel.”",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Who’s behind the amendment that would encode hunting and fishing in the state constitution?",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://kgnu.org/whos-behind-the-amendment-that-would-encode-hunting-and-fishing-in-the-state-constitution/"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": " — KGNU News",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "The ballot language does specifically allow the state to regulate hunting and fishing. But the language also requires officials to prove their decisions are based on sound scientific reasons, public safety, or to preserve future hunting and fishing opportunities.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "This amendment is a lawsuit waiting to happen every time CPW [Colorado Parks and Wildlife] officials make a decision that the hunting community disagrees with.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
